 "Port to Port": {
  "id": "88a59975-14d7-4acb-8a33-ae366d1893ed",
  "title": "Port to Port",
  "subtitle": "A full navigation course, last line to first line",
  "description": "Most navigation training tells you what the rules say. Almost none of it\ntells you what the job feels like at the moment you have to do it — which\norder things happen in, what you write down and where, who you call and\nwhen, and what somebody checks when they step onto your bridge.\n\nPort to Port is that missing conversation. It follows a single voyage from\nthe last line ashore to the first line at the next port, in the order it\nreally happens rather than the order a textbook puts it in: preparation,\ndeparture, the coastal watch, open sea, the ocean passage, arrival\npreparation, and coming alongside.\n\nIt is taught by a serving Second Officer — close enough to your first trip\nto remember what he did not know, and far enough ahead to have learned it.\nEvery lesson ends with something real to do, and you photograph your own\nwork. Nobody marks it. The doing is the part that sticks, and it is also the\neasiest part to skip.\n\nThe course is tech-adaptive: the duty stays, the tool does the arithmetic.\nYou will not be asked to reduce a sight by hand. You will be asked to know\nwhat the answer is for, and what to do when it looks wrong.",
