 "The Other Half": {
  "id": "ad3740fb-62ed-4d5a-b5ce-d51688d659d5",
  "title": "The Other Half",
  "subtitle": "A Third Officer's job, outside the watch",
  "description": "Every third officer is trained to keep a watch. Almost none of them are\ntrained for the rest of the day.\n\nThe rest of the day is the port documents, the crew change, the muster list,\nthe lifeboat inventory, the drill report, the requisition, the bonded store,\nand the inspector standing at the top of the gangway. It is most of the\nworking week, it is what gets a ship detained, and it is the part your name\nends up attached to.\n\nThis course is that missing half. It follows one ship through one month —\na handover in Singapore, a crew change the same week, a month end at sea, a\nport call at the other end and somebody coming aboard to check — in the\norder it actually happens, with the real forms on screen and the real\nreasons behind them.\n\nIt is taught by a working officer who did the job, and it is honest about\nwhere the book and the ship disagree. Where a shortcut exists, you are told\nit exists, told what it costs, and told who has to approve it before you use\nit. Every lesson ends with something real to do, and you photograph your own\nwork. Nobody marks it. The doing is the part that sticks.\n\nMost of this has no regulation behind it, and the course says so. Where a\nconvention settles the question it is named. Where it does not, what you are\ngetting is one officer's practice and the reasoning under it, labelled as\nexactly that.",
